# 🤖 BERT-Base Encoder Block (Complete Step-by-Step Flow)

> **Interview Question:** *"Explain the complete forward pass of one BERT-Base encoder block with dimensions."*

---

# 📌 BERT-Base Configuration

| Parameter | Value |
|-----------|-------|
| Model | BERT-Base |
| Encoder Blocks | 12 |
| Hidden Size ($d_{model}$) | 768 |
| Attention Heads | 12 |
| Head Dimension ($d_k$) | 64 |
| Feed Forward Dimension | 3072 |
| Maximum Sequence Length | 512 |

Head Dimension:

```text
Head Dimension

=

Hidden Size / Number of Heads

=

768 / 12

=

64
```

---

# 📌 Example Input

Sentence:

```text
"I love transformers"
```

After BERT Tokenization:

```text
[CLS] I love transformers [SEP]
```

Total Tokens:

```text
5
```

---

# 📌 Step 1: Embedding Layer

Each token is converted into a **768-dimensional vector**.

BERT adds three embeddings:

```text
Token Embedding

+

Position Embedding

+

Segment Embedding
```

Output:

```text
X

Shape

(5 × 768)
```

Example:

```text
[CLS]

↓

[0.12, -0.43, 0.91, ..., 768 values]
```

---

# 📌 Step 2: Input to Encoder Block

```text
Input

↓

X

Shape

(5 × 768)
```

---

# 📌 Step 3: Compute Query, Key, and Value

Three learned linear layers are applied.

```text
Q = XWq

K = XWk

V = XWv
```

Weight matrices:

```text
Wq

(768 × 768)

Wk

(768 × 768)

Wv

(768 × 768)
```

Output:

```text
Q

(5 × 768)

K

(5 × 768)

V

(5 × 768)
```

---

# 📌 Step 4: Split into 12 Attention Heads

Each head receives:

```text
768 / 12

=

64 dimensions
```

Reshape:

```text
Q

(5 × 12 × 64)

K

(5 × 12 × 64)

V

(5 × 12 × 64)
```

Each head processes attention independently.

---

# 📌 Step 5: Compute Attention Scores

For each attention head:

```text
Attention Scores

=

QKᵀ
```

Matrix multiplication:

```text
(5 × 64)

×

(64 × 5)

↓

(5 × 5)
```

Scale the scores:

```text
QKᵀ

────────

√64

=

QKᵀ

──────

8
```

Scaling prevents excessively large values before Softmax.

---

# 📌 Step 6: Apply Softmax

Softmax is applied row-wise.

Example:

```text
Attention Matrix

(5 × 5)
```

Example row:

```text
[0.10, 0.30, 0.40, 0.10, 0.10]
```

Interpretation:

```text
Current Token

↓

Attends

10%

30%

40%

10%

10%

to all tokens
```

Each row sums to **1**.

---

# 📌 Step 7: Multiply with Value Matrix

```text
Attention

×

V
```

Matrix multiplication:

```text
(5 × 5)

×

(5 × 64)

↓

(5 × 64)
```

Each head produces:

```text
Head Output

(5 × 64)
```

---

# 📌 Step 8: Concatenate All Heads

There are **12 attention heads**.

```text
12 × 64

=

768
```

Concatenate:

```text
Head₁

Head₂

...

Head₁₂

↓

Concatenate

↓

(5 × 768)
```

---

# 📌 Step 9: Final Attention Projection

Apply another linear layer.

Weight matrix:

```text
Wo

(768 × 768)
```

Output:

```text
Attention Output

(5 × 768)
```

---

# 📌 Step 10: Dropout

Apply Dropout.

Typical probability:

```text
0.1
```

Operation:

```text
Attention Output

↓

Dropout

↓

(5 × 768)
```

Shape remains unchanged.

---

# 📌 Step 11: Residual Connection

Add the original input.

```text
Z₁

=

X

+

Attention Output
```

Shape:

```text
(5 × 768)
```

### Why Residual Connections?

They:

- Improve gradient flow
- Reduce vanishing gradients
- Preserve input information
- Enable deeper Transformer models

---

# 📌 Step 12: Layer Normalization

Normalize each token independently.

Formula:

```text
LayerNorm(x)

=

(x − Mean)

──────────────

√(Variance + ε)

×

γ

+

β
```

Output:

```text
Z₁_norm

(5 × 768)
```

### Why LayerNorm?

- Stabilizes training
- Normalizes feature distributions
- Accelerates convergence

---

# 📌 Step 13: Feed Forward Network (First Linear Layer)

Weight matrix:

```text
W₁

(768 × 3072)
```

Matrix multiplication:

```text
(5 × 768)

×

(768 × 3072)

↓

(5 × 3072)
```

The hidden dimension expands from **768** to **3072**.

---

# 📌 Step 14: GELU Activation

BERT uses the **GELU (Gaussian Error Linear Unit)** activation.

```text
FF₁

=

GELU(Z₁_normW₁ + b₁)
```

Output:

```text
(5 × 3072)
```

### Why GELU?

Unlike ReLU, GELU smoothly weights inputs instead of making a hard zero/non-zero decision.

---

# 📌 Step 15: Second Linear Layer

Project back to the hidden size.

Weight matrix:

```text
W₂

(3072 × 768)
```

Matrix multiplication:

```text
(5 × 3072)

×

(3072 × 768)

↓

(5 × 768)
```

---

# 📌 Step 16: Dropout Again

```text
FF₂

↓

Dropout

↓

(5 × 768)
```

---

# 📌 Step 17: Second Residual Connection

```text
Z₂

=

Z₁_norm

+

FF₂
```

Shape:

```text
(5 × 768)
```

---

# 📌 Step 18: Second Layer Normalization

Apply LayerNorm again.

Output:

```text
Encoder Output

↓

(5 × 768)
```

This becomes the input to the **next encoder block**.

---

# 🔄 Complete Encoder Block Pipeline

```text
                 Input
              (5 × 768)
                   │
                   ▼
        Multi-Head Self-Attention
                   │
                   ▼
          Linear Projection (Wo)
                   │
                   ▼
               Dropout
                   │
                   ▼
         Add Residual Connection
                   │
                   ▼
             Layer Normalization
                   │
                   ▼
        Linear Layer (768 → 3072)
                   │
                   ▼
                 GELU
                   │
                   ▼
        Linear Layer (3072 → 768)
                   │
                   ▼
               Dropout
                   │
                   ▼
         Add Residual Connection
                   │
                   ▼
             Layer Normalization
                   │
                   ▼
              Output (5 × 768)
```

